window.__hvBdRegister("plut-moralia-1", {"key":"plut-moralia-1","sections":[{"i":0,"j":14,"loc":"On the education of children 1–4","title":"Good birth, sober begetting, and the power of habit","summary":"The essay opens by asking what makes free-born children grow into men of sound character. Plutarch begins with parentage: fathers should avoid courtesans and concubines, since low birth is a lifelong reproach, and should not beget children when drunk. He then states that moral excellence, like any art, needs nature, reason (learning) and habit (practice) together, comparing them to soil, farmer and seed. Lacking natural gifts can be made good by effort, as the many examples of water wearing rock and tilled land show, and he ends with Lycurgus' two puppies of one litter, raised to become a glutton and a hunter.","ideas":["Honourable birth gives a man freedom of speech; base birth humbles the spirit","Virtue requires nature, learning and practice in combination","Diligence can repair a poor nature, while neglect ruins a good one","Character is habit long continued"],"terms":[{"t":"physis, logos, ethos (nature, reason, habit)","d":"The three things Plutarch says must concur to produce right action."},{"t":"Lycurgus' dogs","d":"Two puppies of one litter whose different rearing showed the Spartans the power of training."}]},{"i":15,"j":35,"loc":"On the education of children 5–9","title":"Nurses, attendants, teachers, and the danger of showy speech","summary":"Mothers should nurse their own infants, or else choose good Greek nurses, since a child's character is soft wax that takes every impression. Slave companions and attendants must be of sound character, not the drunken and useless slaves fathers commonly assign; teachers above all must be of blameless life and proven skill, not chosen for cheapness or to please friends, as Aristippus' jibe about buying two slaves shows. Badly raised sons fall into flatterers, courtesans and dice, while good education is the beginning, middle and end of virtue and happiness, since birth, wealth, beauty and strength are all unstable and learning alone is immortal. Plutarch then warns against training boys in crowd-pleasing extempore speaking, urging prepared speech, like that of Pericles and Demosthenes, and a style that keeps to the middle course.","ideas":["Young minds take impressions like soft wax, so their first companions matter","Fathers who economize on teachers neglect the foot while caring for the shoe","Education is the one possession that war, disease and age cannot take","Extempore speaking breeds rambling; reflection keeps speech in proportion"],"terms":[{"t":"paideia (education)","d":"Proper training, which Plutarch calls the source and root of all goodness."},{"t":"Stilpo's answer","d":"Told by Demetrius he had lost everything, Stilpo replied that war cannot plunder virtue."}]},{"i":36,"j":50,"loc":"On the education of children 10–13","title":"Philosophy first, exercise, and gentle correction","summary":"The free-born child should taste every branch of general education but honour philosophy above all, as one may visit many cities but should live in the best. Philosophy alone heals the soul, teaching duties to gods, parents, laws, friends and slaves, and the ideal man joins public life with philosophy, like Pericles, Archytas, Dion and Epaminondas. Plutarch commends collecting books, moderate bodily training aimed at war, and answers the objection that his advice suits only the rich. Children should be led by praise and reproof rather than blows, not crushed by excessive tasks, given rest as well as labour, checked on personally by their fathers, and trained in memory, the mother of the Muses.","ideas":["Philosophy is the head of education, as medicine and gymnastics serve the body","The best life combines the practical and the contemplative","Free children should be corrected by encouragement, not beatings","Rest gives relish to labour; memory is the storehouse of learning"],"terms":[{"t":"three lives","d":"The practical, contemplative and pleasure-seeking lives, of which Plutarch rejects the last and wants the first two combined."},{"t":"Mnemosyne (Memory)","d":"Called mother of the Muses, which Plutarch reads as an allegory of memory's power to foster learning."}]},{"i":51,"j":89,"loc":"On the education of children 14–20","title":"Self-control, boy-love, adolescence, flatterers and fathers","summary":"Boys must be kept from foul language, taught tact, and trained in four rules: simple living, a held tongue, mastered anger and clean hands, illustrated by Gylippus, Socrates kicked by a youth, Archytas and Plato restraining their temper, and the fatal jokes of Sotades and Theocritus. Children must be taught truthfulness. Plutarch admits he is divided on whether boys' admirers should be allowed, but would admit lovers of the soul on the Athenian and Spartan pattern. Adolescence needs closer watching than childhood; Pythagorean symbols are explained as moral maxims; flatterers and bad schoolmates must be driven off. Fathers should be mild, sometimes overlook faults, marry headstrong sons to equals, and above all be examples, like Eurydice who learned letters for her sons.","ideas":["Timely silence is wiser than any speech; a spoken word cannot be recalled","Faults of youth are graver than those of childhood and need firmer reins","Pythagoras' sayings, such as 'Abstain from beans', hide practical precepts","A father's own life is the mirror his children study"],"terms":[{"t":"Pythagorean symbols","d":"Riddling precepts, like 'Do not poke a fire with steel', which Plutarch interprets as advice on conduct."},{"t":"kolax (flatterer)","d":"The most pernicious corrupter of rich young men, urging pleasure against a father's counsel."}]},{"i":90,"j":132,"loc":"How the young man should study poetry 1–2","title":"Guiding the young reader: poetry mixes truth and fiction","summary":"Writing to Marcus Sedatus about their sons Soclarus and Cleander, Plutarch says the young delight in fables, so their reading must be governed like their eating. Poetry, like Egypt, yields both medicines and poisons; rather than stop the ears as Epicurus would, one should prune its wild growth and blend it with philosophy, using it as a preparation for philosophic study. The first principle is that poets mix truth with falsehood, since fiction is what makes poetry charming; even Socrates versified Aesop because poetry needs fable. Some fictions poets invent knowingly, like Zeus' scales of fate, while others express their own mistaken beliefs about the gods or the dead; since even philosophers cannot settle these matters, the young need not trust poets on them.","ideas":["Poetry should be tempered with philosophy, not banished","Fiction, not metre, is what makes a work poetry","Grim pictures of the underworld are fable mixed with belief, like poison in food","Where even philosophers are uncertain, poets deserve little credence"],"terms":[{"t":"mythos (fable, fiction)","d":"The invented story which Plutarch says is essential to poetry and the source of its charm."},{"t":"Psychostasia","d":"Aeschylus' tragedy built on Homer's image of Zeus weighing heroes' fates."}]},{"i":133,"j":201,"loc":"How the young man should study poetry 3–4","title":"Poetry as imitation, and correcting poets by poets","summary":"Poetry is an imitative art like painting: we praise a picture of Medea killing her children for its likeness, not its subject, and so the young must admire a poet's skill in giving wicked speeches to wicked characters without approving them. Plutarch shows how poets signal disapproval in words, as Homer frames Agamemnon's and Achilles' outbursts with censure, or in deeds, as Euripides bound Ixion to the wheel. He rejects physical allegories of Ares and Aphrodite and Hera's seduction of Zeus, reading them instead as moral lessons. Where poets contradict themselves, the reader should side with the better saying, and where they do not, quote other poets or philosophers against them, as Diogenes answered Sophocles on the mysteries.","ideas":["The beauty of an imitation differs from the goodness of what is imitated","Homer passes judgment on bad speeches and acts before or after giving them","Homer's myths teach morals better than allegorists' astronomical readings","Set a poet's better sayings, or a sage's, against his worse ones"],"terms":[{"t":"mimēsis (imitation)","d":"The art of representing, praised for likeness and propriety rather than for the goodness of its subject."},{"t":"hyponoiai (allegories)","d":"The ancients' word for hidden meanings, which Plutarch thinks the allegorists force on Homer."}]},{"i":202,"j":258,"loc":"How the young man should study poetry 5–6","title":"Reading words aright: gods, fate, virtue, happiness","summary":"Plutarch recommends using nearby words to soften a suspect line and learning the varied senses of words, giving examples of Greek terms with several meanings. Above all the young must learn how poets use the names of gods: 'Hephaestus' may mean the god or simply fire, 'Ares' the god, war or bronze weapons, and 'Zeus' the god himself, fortune or fate. Read this way, Homer's Zeus contriving woes means the necessity of events, and Hesiod's gifts of Zeus mean fortune's goods. Likewise 'virtue' in poets often means glory or power, 'evil' may mean misfortune, and 'happiness' is often loosely applied to the rich, unlike its strict philosophical sense.","ideas":["One word can carry several senses, and context decides","Divine names in poetry may stand for natural forces, fortune or fate","Poetic 'virtue' and 'happiness' often mean mere reputation or wealth","Apparently impious lines can be rescued by careful reading"],"terms":[{"t":"aretē (virtue)","d":"Properly right reason and the excellence of rational nature, but used by poets also for honour and power."},{"t":"eudaimonia (happiness)","d":"In philosophy a complete life in accord with nature; in poets often a loose name for wealth or power."}]},{"i":259,"j":293,"loc":"How the young man should study poetry 7–9","title":"Mixed characters: judging heroes' words and deeds","summary":"Poetry imitates life, where virtue and vice are mixed, so Homer, unlike the Stoics, shows good men erring and even gods with passions, which also gives poetry its variety. The young must therefore not admire every word of heroes as models of wisdom, but learn to say plainly what was well or ill done. Plutarch weighs Achilles' speeches and drawn sword, Agamemnon's handling of Chryseis, Phoenix's near-parricide, Nausicaa's wish, and Odysseus counting his treasure, judging each by its motive. Plausible pleas for bad acts in tragedy, such as Phaedra's or Helen's, should be loathed more than the acts, and like the boy Cato the reader should ask the reason for every saying.","ideas":["Homer's heroes mix virtue and vice, contrary to Stoic doctrine","The same act may be blameworthy or praiseworthy depending on motive","Clever speeches defending wrongdoing are especially dangerous","Poets, unlike lawgivers, must give reasons to be obeyed"],"terms":[{"t":"Stoic paradox","d":"The school doctrine that the non-wise do nothing well and the wise nothing amiss, which Homer's poetry ignores."}]},{"i":294,"j":330,"loc":"How the young man should study poetry 10","title":"Learning character from Homer's contrasts","summary":"Having shown how to avoid harm from poetry, Plutarch turns to its hidden benefits, beginning with contrasts of character. Achilles speaks to Agamemnon with restraint while Thersites rails; Diomedes bears reproach in silence while Sthenelus boasts; Calchas accuses the king before the crowd while Nestor counsels him tactfully. The Greeks advance in disciplined silence while the Trojans shout, Dolon boasts while Diomedes asks for a companion, and Ajax praises his fellows. The section ends noting that no Greek, unlike many Trojans, begs an enemy for mercy.","ideas":["Modesty becomes the noble, boasting the base","Tact in correcting a ruler differs from public accusation","Fear of disgrace among soldiers shows courage and discipline","Greeks are shown preferring to conquer or die rather than plead"],"terms":[]},{"i":331,"j":400,"loc":"How the young man should study poetry 11–12","title":"Moral lessons drawn and bad lines corrected","summary":"As bees, goats and pigs take different parts of a plant, the reader seeking character should note passages on valour, temperance and justice: gods love Odysseus for his wisdom, Achilles guards himself against his own anger before Priam, and Odysseus forearms Telemachus against rage. Plutarch dismisses the fanciful etymologies of Cleanthes and Chrysippus and draws lessons that courage can be taught and wisdom is the most kingly quality. Even suspect passages teach something, such as Agamemnon taking a mare in place of a coward. He then commends the practice of Antisthenes, Cleanthes and Zeno of amending poets' lines and offers his own corrections, ending with the line that piety strengthens courage.","ideas":["Wisdom is presented as the root of continence, courage and all virtue","Forestalling passion is greater than mastering it once roused","A bad line can be turned into a good one by a small change","Fear of the gods belongs only to the foolish and ungrateful"],"terms":[{"t":"epanorthōsis (correction)","d":"Rewriting a poet's line so that it states a truer moral, as Zeno did with Sophocles."}]},{"i":401,"j":448,"loc":"How the young man should study poetry 13–14","title":"Extending poets' sayings and joining them to philosophy","summary":"Following Chrysippus, Plutarch advises applying a useful line to similar cases, as a doctor uses one drug for related diseases. Homer's epithets show he values character over beauty or wealth: heroes are greeted for wisdom, insults target vices rather than bodily defects, and Hera even teases Hephaestus about his lameness. The reader thus learns not to mock misfortune and to bear mockery calmly. Finally, poets' sound sayings should be matched with philosophers' doctrines, such as Know thyself, Plato on doing wrong, and Epicurus on short griefs, so that poetry, stripped of fable, eases the young into philosophy like eyes adjusting to light.","ideas":["A saying fitted to one case can be transferred to its like","Homer praises and blames for virtue and vice, not body or fortune","Poets' maxims often anticipate philosophers' doctrines","Poetry accustoms the young to truths they will later meet in philosophy"],"terms":[{"t":"Know thyself","d":"The Delphic maxim Plutarch finds restated in Zeus' warning to Aphrodite to leave war alone."}]},{"i":449,"j":465,"loc":"On listening to lectures 1–7","title":"Hearing well: silence, envy, and misplaced admiration","summary":"Writing to Nicander, newly come of age, Plutarch says manhood means exchanging hired guardians for reason as one's guide, not casting off control. Hearing is the sense most open to emotion yet the only hold virtue has on the young, so it must be guarded, and listening must be learned before speaking, since we have two ears and one tongue. The good hearer keeps silent, waits before objecting, and does not let envy spoil a good discourse. He should learn from a speaker's successes and failures, testing himself by trying to improve on what he heard, but should be generous in praising style while cautious in believing doctrine, unmoved by grey hair, applause or showy delivery.","ideas":["To follow God and to obey reason are the same","Reception of discourse must come before its delivery","Envy of a speaker hurts the listener's own good","Praise the speaker freely, but test his claims strictly"],"terms":[{"t":"akroasis (listening)","d":"Attentive hearing of lectures, which Plutarch treats as an art needing practice."}]},{"i":466,"j":483,"loc":"On listening to lectures 8–13","title":"Seeking substance, asking questions, giving due praise","summary":"Like bees rather than garland-makers, the student should pass over ornate style to the useful matter and judge a lecture by whether it has lessened his passions and troubles, as one checks a haircut in a mirror. Pleasure in style may come later, once he is filled with doctrine. Questions should be few, useful, suited to the speaker's field, and not petty displays of logic; one should bring one's own urgent faults to be treated rather than enjoy hearing others rebuked. Commendation must avoid both grudging silence and indiscriminate applause, since nearly every speaker offers something to praise, and the hearer should sit attentively without fidgeting or yawning.","ideas":["A discourse, like a bath, is useless unless it cleanses","Concern with Attic style over substance breeds empty schoolroom chatter","Ask about what truly afflicts you, not frivolous puzzles","Wonder at nothing does not mean praising nothing"],"terms":[{"t":"Pythagoras' maxim","d":"To wonder at nothing, which Plutarch says some wrongly take as licence to withhold all praise."}]},{"i":484,"j":498,"loc":"On listening to lectures 14–18","title":"The hearer's duties, rebuke, and independent thought","summary":"The hearer is a fellow-worker with the speaker, like the catcher in a ball game, and should not bring outlandish cries like 'Divine!' into the lecture-room. Rebukes should be met neither with grinning indifference nor by fleeing philosophy, but endured like surgery or an initiation's first purifications. Philosophy's difficulties at the outset yield to persistence; students err both by shame that keeps them from asking and by vanity that claims understanding too soon, while Cleanthes and Xenocrates bore mockery and kept what they learned. Equally, one must not pester the speaker with repeated or idle questions, but use what is heard as a seed, since the mind needs kindling, not filling like a bottle.","ideas":["Listening carries obligations just as speaking does","Reproof's sting is healed by the same words that caused it","The mind is a fire to kindle, not a vessel to fill","Right listening is the beginning of right living"],"terms":[]},{"i":499,"j":525,"loc":"How to tell a flatterer from a friend 1–8","title":"Why flattery is hard to detect; the shifting imitator","summary":"Self-love, the flatterer within, makes us welcome flatterers from without, who defy Apollo's Know thyself. Flattery preys on great men and great houses, so a friend must be tested like coin before he is needed, though a pleasant friend should not be suspected merely for being pleasant. The dangerous flatterer is not the table-parasite but one who imitates a friend gravely, copying his pleasantness, services, likeness of tastes and even frank speech. The first test is consistency: the flatterer changes shape with his company, as Alcibiades did at Athens, Sparta, Thrace and Persia, while Epaminondas, Agesilaus and Plato stayed the same everywhere, and one can expose him by praising and blaming the same thing in turn.","ideas":["Self-love prepares the way for outside flattery","The most dangerous flatterer mimics friendship seriously","A true friend's character is constant; the flatterer's mirrors his company","Pretending to change one's views reveals a flatterer who follows"],"terms":[{"t":"parrhēsia (frankness)","d":"The free speech proper to friendship, which the cleverest flatterer imitates."},{"t":"polypus","d":"The octopus, whose changing colour Plutarch compares to the flatterer's shifting opinions."}]},{"i":526,"j":552,"loc":"How to tell a flatterer from a friend 9–12","title":"Copying vices, yielding first, pleasing, and false praise","summary":"A friend imitates only what deserves it, while the flatterer copies vices and even feigns infirmities, like Dionysius' courtiers pretending blindness. Where he imitates good qualities he always yields the prize, though in vices he outdoes his patron. Both friend and flatterer please, but to different ends: the friend uses pleasure as sauce and will cause pain when needed, like a physician, as Menedemus and Arcesilaus did, while the flatterer keeps playing on one string. Praise must be tested by asking whether it would be given to the absent and whether conscience agrees; the worst flattery renames vices as virtues, which ruined Sicily, Egypt and nearly Rome under Antony.","ideas":["The flatterer imitates faults, the friend virtues","A friend grants some things and refuses others, as a musician tunes strings","Praise that renames vice as virtue corrupts the root of conduct","Flattery of rulers has destroyed whole kingdoms"],"terms":[{"t":"chameleon","d":"Takes every colour but white, as the flatterer copies every quality but goodness."}]},{"i":553,"j":563,"loc":"How to tell a flatterer from a friend 13–16","title":"Indirect praise and silent flattery","summary":"With perceptive men, the flatterer praises indirectly: reporting praise from strangers, inventing a charge for the pleasure of denying it, mocking virtues opposite to his patron's vices, or running himself down to lift another. He asks advice from those proud of their judgment and submits writings for correction; Plutarch advises testing him with absurd advice and seeing if he applauds. There is also silent flattery: giving place, yielding the floor and retracting opinions before the rich and powerful. Apelles with Megabyzus and Solon with Croesus show honest speech to the great, while Carneades notes that princes learn only riding well, because the horse does not flatter.","ideas":["Flattery often works through feigned accusation or self-abasement","Deference paid to wealth rather than worth reveals a flatterer","Only the horse treats a prince like anyone else","Excessive praise puffs a man up and ruins him"],"terms":[]},{"i":564,"j":600,"loc":"How to tell a flatterer from a friend 17–25","title":"Counterfeit frankness, base services, and slander of friends","summary":"The flatterer's frankness is like Menander's sham Heracles with a hollow club: he rebukes trifles while ignoring real crimes, or blames in ways that compliment, as with Tiberius' senator, or charges a miser with prodigality, or inflames quarrels, as Antony's friends did over Cleopatra. The friend addresses the rational part of the soul, the flatterer the passions. In services, the flatterer is effusive, promises anything, endorses any plan, and advertises his trouble, while friends like Arcesilaus and Lacydes help secretly. The flatterer serves base ends but shirks hard ones, and slanders his patron's real friends, as Medius did at Alexander's court. Plutarch concludes that self-knowledge is the remedy, and that frankness must avoid both flattery and harshness.","ideas":["The flatterer's rebuke tickles and provokes rather than heals","A friend's kindnesses are quiet; a flatterer's are noisy and upbraided","A friend helps in honest causes only; a flatterer in base ones","Each vice is corrected by the mean, not by the opposite extreme"],"terms":[{"t":"Know thyself","d":"The oracle Plutarch again offers as the defence against flattery, by honest self-assessment."}]},{"i":601,"j":617,"loc":"How to tell a flatterer from a friend 26–29","title":"Frank speech: free of self-interest, scorn and bad timing","summary":"Plutarch now treats the right use of frankness. Rebuke must be free of personal grievance, as Odysseus' reproof was accepted by Agamemnon, and Plato pleaded for Aeschines before Dionysius rather than for himself. It must avoid mockery and biting jests, which cost Antiphon his life, and should not be offered at drinking parties. Friends in misfortune need comfort, not reproach, as Crates consoled the exiled Demetrius of Phalerum, while friends in prosperity need checking. Severe rebuke is right when a friend is carried away by pleasure, anger or pride, as Solon warned Croesus and Socrates moved Alcibiades to tears.","ideas":["Disinterested reproof inspires respect; complaint breeds resentment","Scurrilous wit destroys a rebuke's force and the rebuker","The fortunate need correction; the unfortunate need consolation","Reserve severity for a friend's serious excesses"],"terms":[]},{"i":618,"j":658,"loc":"How to tell a flatterer from a friend 30–37","title":"The art of reproof: occasion, privacy, praise, and healing","summary":"A friend may seize occasions for frankness from a question or a story, or after a third party has attacked, or by rebuking someone else, as Plutarch's teacher Ammonius had his own son whipped to reprove his overfed pupils. Reproof belongs in private, as Socrates' retort to Plato and the deaths of Clitus and Aristomenes show, and the rebuker should live well himself or include himself in the fault. Praise should temper blame, comparison should be with the friend's own past or parents, not rivals, and one should not retort on a monitor or carp at trifles. Past faults call for gentle excuse, but faults to be prevented call for vehement warning, and the essay ends advising that every rebuke be followed by kindly talk, as surgeons soothe a wound.","ideas":["Correct a friend in private, not as a public show","Sharing the blame makes a rebuke acceptable","Mix praise with reproof, as iron is softened then tempered","Never leave a friend smarting after admonition"],"terms":[{"t":"Ammonius","d":"Plutarch's own teacher, who rebuked his students indirectly by punishing his son."}]},{"i":659,"j":676,"loc":"How a man may become aware of his progress in virtue 1–4","title":"Against the Stoics: progress in virtue is perceptible","summary":"Plutarch attacks the Stoic thesis that everyone short of the perfect sage is equally vicious, so that a man could become wise overnight without noticing. Siding with Plato, he holds that vice and virtue admit of degrees and that the soul's ascent can be counted step by step. The first signs of progress are steady, uninterrupted application, like a ship's run measured by time and wind, and a resolve that grows stronger rather than flagging, like a cane whose joints crowd together near the top. A true lover of philosophy is restless when kept from it, as a lover is in the beloved's absence.","ideas":["Stoic doctrine that all non-sages are equally bad is refuted by their own practice","Progress has degrees and can be observed","Constancy and growing intensity of effort are signs of advance","Longing for philosophy when away from it marks real attachment"],"terms":[{"t":"prokopē (progress)","d":"Moral advancement toward virtue, which Plutarch holds is gradual and measurable."},{"t":"Stoic paradox of equal vice","d":"The claim that all who fall short of perfect wisdom are equally wicked, which Plutarch rejects."}]},{"i":677,"j":693,"loc":"How a man may become aware of his progress in virtue 5–9","title":"Firmness, changed reading, and calm debate","summary":"Further signs follow: the way grows smoother, as Hesiod says, and lapses become rarer and shorter; Sextius' despair and Diogenes' lesson from the mouse show the early struggle. The proficient is unmoved by friends' worldly success, valuing virtue above wealth with Solon, Agesilaus and Zeno. His reading shifts from style and display to what forms character, gathering like Simonides' bee and drawing lessons from every sight, as Aeschylus, Brasidas and Diogenes did. In argument he prefers learning to winning and keeps his composure whether audiences are large or small.","ideas":["Rare and brief lapses show advance","Indifference to others' wealth and office","Read for substance, not style, like the bee","Debate without vehemence; Aristippus sleeps better than his victor"],"terms":[{"t":"Simonides' bee","d":"The image of the student who extracts useful moral 'honey' from poetry rather than enjoying its colour and scent."}]},{"i":694,"j":710,"loc":"How a man may become aware of his progress in virtue 10–11","title":"Virtue without display; welcoming reproof","summary":"The proficient acts for profit rather than show, keeping his good deeds and private self-restraint to himself and content with his own conscience as witness. Like full ears of corn that bend, the filled mind loses arrogance; Menedemus says students at Athens went from 'wise' to plain men. Openness to correction is a further sign: the hopeful man seeks out reproof and confesses his faults, unlike those who parade bodily defects but hide vices of soul. Hippocrates' public admission of error is praised, while the maxims of Bion and Pyrrhon are said to describe completed virtue rather than progress.","ideas":["Good deeds kept quiet show reason rooted within","Learning breeds humility, emptiness breeds pride","Seeking and accepting reproof marks curable souls","Hippocrates confessed his error on skull sutures"],"terms":[{"t":"philosophos","d":"Lover of wisdom; Menedemus' joke traces students' descent from claiming to be wise to becoming plain honest men."}]},{"i":711,"j":730,"loc":"How a man may become aware of his progress in virtue 12–17","title":"Dreams, passions, emulation, and care for small faults","summary":"Following Zeno and Plato, Plutarch takes calm and decent dreams as a sign that the irrational part has been tamed; Stilpo's bold answer to Poseidon in a dream illustrates it. Progress also shows in the moderation of passions, measured against their former strength and against each other. The proficient wishes to imitate what he praises, as Themistocles was spurred by Miltiades, loves good men even in misfortune, and pictures what Plato or Epaminondas would do. He is at ease before good men and treats every small fault as serious, finishing the building of character with care.","ideas":["Dreams reveal the state of the irrational soul","Passions grow milder and exchange for gentler ones","Admiration must become imitation","Small faults matter once a sound foundation is laid"],"terms":[{"t":"apatheia (freedom from passion)","d":"A divine perfection; progress consists in the gradual moderation of passions toward it."}]},{"i":731,"j":767,"loc":"How to profit by one's enemies 1–7","title":"An enemy's watchfulness as moral training","summary":"Addressed to Cornelius Pulcher, the essay takes up Xenophon's remark that sensible men profit even from enemies. As men learned to use wild beasts, so we can use a foe: his constant prying forces us to live circumspectly, as border wars keep states orderly. The best way to vex an enemy is to be good oneself, and anyone who reviles another must first be free of the fault, or he will hear it thrown back. Being reviled is also useful, since enemies tell truths friends withhold, and even false charges should send us to look for what gave rise to them.","ideas":["An enemy's scrutiny compels careful living","Answer an enemy by being honourable","Reproach only what you are free of yourself","Enemies speak truths friends conceal"],"terms":[{"t":"Know thyself","d":"The Delphic precept, said to apply above all to one who means to censure another."}]},{"i":768,"j":791,"loc":"How to profit by one's enemies 8–11","title":"Silence, generosity, and rivalry with enemies","summary":"Bearing an enemy's abuse in silence trains the temper, so that one can bear a wife or brother calmly, as Socrates practised on Xanthippe. Fairness to an enemy, helping him in need and praising his merits, as Caesar restored Pompey's statues, removes envy and keeps deceit from becoming a habit used against friends; Scaurus and Cato are examples. Contentiousness, which all men have, is best drained off onto enemies, like garlic planted beside roses, and turned into emulation of their diligence. Their dishonourable gains should be judged against our own freedom and virtue.","ideas":["Silence under abuse is a school of self-command","Justice toward enemies protects friendships","Divert envy and rivalry onto enemies","Study an enemy's successes to imitate, his failings to avoid"],"terms":[]},{"i":792,"j":846,"loc":"On having many friends 1–9","title":"Why true friendship cannot be shared widely","summary":"Starting from Socrates' retort to Meno's swarm of virtues, Plutarch argues that those who lack one secure friendship should not seek many. Friendship pairs, like Theseus and Peirithoüs, and needs goodwill, intimacy and usefulness tested over long time, the proverbial bushel of salt. Many friends cannot all be tried, enjoyed or served; conflicting demands breed jealousy, and friends bring their enemies and misfortunes with them. Above all friendship requires likeness of character, and adapting to many people would need a Proteus or a cuttle-fish, whereas friendship seeks a fixed and steadfast nature.","ideas":["Friendship is properly between a few, a pair","Friends must be tested slowly before acceptance","Many friends mean many claims, enmities and griefs","Friendship rests on likeness of character"],"terms":[{"t":"philia (friendship)","d":"A bond of goodwill, intimacy and use, which Plutarch thinks is weakened when divided among many."}]},{"i":847,"j":904,"loc":"On Fortune 1–6; Virtue and Vice 1–4","title":"Intelligence, not chance, rules life; virtue brings contentment","summary":"On Fortune rejects the verse that human affairs are chance: if justice and self-control are real, so is the intelligence they presuppose. Man, naked and unarmed beside the beasts, masters them by reason; the arts rely on rule and measure, chance helping only rarely, as with the painter's sponge. Intelligence is what makes wealth and strength useful. The short Virtue and Vice adds that, as clothes only keep in a man's own warmth, happiness comes from character, not possessions: vice troubles even sleep, while reason makes plain living pleasant.","ideas":["Ascribing virtue to chance abolishes deliberation","Reason, not nature's gifts, gives man mastery","Intelligence makes external goods useful","Vice cannot be divorced like a bad wife"],"terms":[{"t":"tychē (chance, fortune)","d":"Luck or accident, which Plutarch denies is the governor of human conduct."},{"t":"phronēsis (sagacity)","d":"Practical intelligence, said to underlie self-control, justice and courage."}]},{"i":905,"j":1043,"loc":"Letter of condolence to Apollonius 1–8","title":"Moderate grief and the changes of fortune","summary":"Plutarch writes to Apollonius some time after his son's death, now that grief has had time to ease. He rejects both callous insensibility and limitless mourning, preferring moderate feeling governed by reason, which prepares us to bear good and ill alike. A stream of poetic quotations, from Menander, Pindar, Euripides and Homer, shows that human fortunes turn like a wheel and that man is a dream of a shadow; Crantor and Theophrastus speak of life's built-in hardship. Homer's two urns of Zeus, Hesiod's Pandora and verses on the uselessness of tears close the section.","ideas":["Grief is natural but must have limits","Reason and foresight are the remedy for sorrow","Fortune constantly reverses human states","Tears do not change events"],"terms":[{"t":"metriopatheia (moderate feeling)","d":"The ideal of measured emotion, between insensibility and excess, that the letter commends."}]},{"i":1044,"j":1091,"loc":"Letter of condolence to Apollonius 9–13","title":"Death is no evil: sleep or journey","summary":"Comparing our misfortunes with those of others lightens them, as Socrates' common store and Antimachus' Lyde show. Aeschylus and others call death a healer, and Heracleitus and the image of clay remodelled show that birth and death are one cycle; life is a debt owed to destiny. Nature hides the hour of death, and poets confirm life's toil. Socrates held death to be sleep, journey or extinction and no evil in any case: sleep is sweet and akin to death, and a long passage of Plato's Phaedo presents death as the soul's release from the body to pure knowledge.","ideas":["Others' ills put our own in proportion","Life is a loan to be repaid cheerfully","Socrates' three possibilities for death","Plato: the body hinders knowledge; death frees the soul"],"terms":[{"t":"Socrates' trilemma","d":"Death is either deep sleep, a migration, or annihilation, and none of these is an evil."}]},{"i":1092,"j":1153,"loc":"Letter of condolence to Apollonius 14–16","title":"Death as divine gift; extinction; untimely death","summary":"Tales show the gods granting death as a reward: Cleobis and Biton, Agamedes and Trophonius, Pindar's oracle, and Elysius' vision of his dead son Euthynoüs, told it was not good for him to live. If death is extinction, it is still no evil, since we return to the state before birth, when nothing was good or bad for us; Arcesilaus notes that death pains only when expected. Poets on Croesus and Xerxes show all go to Hades. Against grief at untimely death, the poets reply that no one knows whether longer life would have been a blessing.","ideas":["Myths of death as the gods' best boon","After death as before birth: no good or evil","Death hurts only in anticipation","Untimely death may spare greater ills"],"terms":[]},{"i":1154,"j":1204,"loc":"Letter of condolence to Apollonius 17–26","title":"The measure of life is excellence, not length","summary":"The best life is the most excellent, not the longest; heroes dear to the gods die young, and beside eternity long and short lose their difference, as with the one-day creatures of the Black Sea. We come to obey the gods, not to legislate. Mourning for the untimely dead is either selfish or mistaken, and if it must end with time it should end now through reason, as Theseus prepared himself for ills. Plato counsels calm; mourning is called womanish and barbarian; Priam's speech to Hector shows how long life brings greater woes; true love honours the dead by memory, not lamentation.","ideas":["Excellence, not years, measures a life","Grief that time will end should be ended by reason","Foreseeing misfortune blunts its blow","Honour the dead with memory, not wailing"],"terms":[{"t":"aōros (untimely)","d":"Dying before one's time; Plutarch argues the notion is relative and offers no ground for grief."}]},{"i":1205,"j":1258,"loc":"Letter of condolence to Apollonius 27–31","title":"Silenus' wisdom and the gods' loan of life","summary":"Crantor and many sages lamented man's lot; Plutarch quotes Aristotle's Eudemus, where Silenus tells Midas that best is not to be born, and next best to die quickly. Since evils outnumber goods, the young dead lose nothing certain; life is a deposit from the gods to be returned without complaint, as honest bankers repay. The Delphic 'Know thyself' and 'Avoid extremes' contain all else, and Pythagoreans and tragedians urge bearing fate without anger at the gods. Mourners find grievance in every manner of death, but God's fatherly foresight may remove some early.","ideas":["Silenus to Midas: best not to be born","Life is held on deposit from the gods","The two Delphic maxims govern grief","Providence may take the young to spare them"],"terms":[{"t":"Mēden agan (Avoid extremes)","d":"Delphic maxim paired with Know thyself as the rule for bearing loss."}]},{"i":1259,"j":1303,"loc":"Letter of condolence to Apollonius 32–37","title":"Exemplary fathers, the blessed dead, and farewell","summary":"Since life is short, grief should not be postponed but cured now by reason. Examples follow of fathers who bore their sons' deaths nobly: Anaxagoras, Pericles, Xenophon finishing his sacrifice for Gryllus, Dion, Demosthenes and Antigonus. Plutarch praises the young Apollonius as loved by the gods, quotes Pindar on the happy dead, and gives Socrates' myth from the Gorgias of the judgement of naked souls by Minos, Rhadamanthys and Aeacus. He closes by urging Apollonius to return to his natural life, as his son would wish.","ideas":["Great men mastered grief for their sons","Whom the gods love dies young","The just dead dwell in the Isles of the Blest","Souls are judged stripped of body and status"],"terms":[{"t":"Islands of the Blest","d":"The abode of the righteous dead in the Gorgias myth, opposed to Tartarus."}]},{"i":1304,"j":1337,"loc":"Advice about keeping well 1–7","title":"Philosophers on health; moderation at feasts","summary":"Moschion and Zeuxippus discuss how the physician Glaucus mocked philosophers for discussing health; Zeuxippus argues that medicine and philosophy share one field. He reports the derided precepts: keep the hands warm, grow used to invalid fare while healthy, and prepare for feasts by eating sparingly beforehand, as Philip told his friends to leave room for cake. One should refuse excess politely rather than die of shame, like Alexander or Regulus. Following Socrates, one should not eat without hunger or for rarity's sake, and plain fare guards the body better than chefs' artful dishes.","ideas":["Health is a proper subject for philosophy","Habituate the body to simple fare in health","Refusing excess is better than false politeness","Pleasures excited by the mind harm the body"],"terms":[{"t":"diaita (regimen)","d":"One's daily way of living in food, drink, exercise and rest, the subject of the dialogue."}]},{"i":1338,"j":1350,"loc":"Advice about keeping well 8–15","title":"Health as the sauce of pleasure; heeding warnings","summary":"The speaker argues that even lovers of pleasure should prize health, since disease spoils pleasures more than anything; health is the best sauce. In illness we should remember the brief indulgences that caused it, as Lysimachus did over his drink of water. Overfeeding gives external causes of disease material to work on. Premonitory signs such as heaviness, lassitude, odd dreams and moods must be heeded rather than ignored out of shame; an over-exact regimen is also harmful. Visiting the sick, one should learn the causes of their illness and correct oneself.","ideas":["Pleasure depends on a healthy body","Excess food feeds outside causes of disease","Heed early symptoms instead of hiding them","Learn from friends' illnesses"],"terms":[]},{"i":1351,"j":1360,"loc":"Advice about keeping well 16–20","title":"Exercise of the voice, baths, food and wine","summary":"For scholars the best exercise is daily use of the voice in speaking and reading aloud, which warms and clears the body, though straining is dangerous, as Niger's fatal lecture shows. Massage with oil is recommended; cold baths are showy and harmful, warm baths sometimes useful. Light foods are preferred to meat, which should be only a prop of the diet. Wine is excellent in moderation with plenty of water, which is better after exertion. At late dinners a scholar can master appetite with problems, books and music.","ideas":["Speaking aloud as the scholar's exercise","Avoid cold baths and heavy meat","Drink water alongside and instead of wine","Turn the mind from appetite to study"],"terms":[]},{"i":1361,"j":1371,"loc":"Advice about keeping well 20–27","title":"Table talk, purges, activity, and self-knowledge","summary":"Light conversation on history, poetry or nature suits dinner, but not hard logic; after dinner the body should be kept warm and quiet with the mind gently occupied. Emetics and purges are condemned as fostering greed; moderate living keeps the body self-regulating. Rigid fasting schedules are rejected, and health is not bought by idleness: public men should save their strength for important tasks and not swing between toil and indulgence. Each should know his own pulse and constitution, and scholars must not overwork the body, for health's best gift is the chance to practise virtue.","ideas":["Pleasant, not contentious, talk at table","Purges and fixed fasts violate nature","Health serves activity, not idleness","Know your own body; balance mind and body as Plato advised"],"terms":[]},{"i":1372,"j":1409,"loc":"Advice to Bride and Groom, prologue–30 (138–142)","title":"Marriage precepts: concord, character, and persuasion","summary":"Plutarch sends Pollianus and Eurydice a wedding gift of brief comparisons on marriage, invoking Hermes, Persuasion and the Graces beside Aphrodite. The precepts urge patience with early quarrels, warn that love built on physical attraction burns out unless it takes hold of character, and reject love-potions, as Circe gained nothing from men turned into swine. Through images of the moon, the mirror, the Sun and the North Wind, and wine mixed with water, he argues that husband and wife should share friends, gods, moods and property, with the husband leading by reason rather than force. The wife's true adornment is dignity and modesty, not gold, and her virtue should be joined with cheerfulness and grace.","ideas":["Lasting love rests on character and reason, not beauty or charms","Persuasion succeeds where force fails","Married life should hold everything in common","Adornment is what makes a woman more decorous"],"terms":[{"t":"Peithō (Persuasion)","d":"Placed beside Aphrodite so that spouses win their desires by persuasion, not quarrelling."},{"t":"kosmos (adornment)","d":"Following Crates, what truly adorns: dignity, good behaviour and modesty."}]},{"i":1410,"j":1444,"loc":"Advice to Bride and Groom 31–48 (142–146)","title":"Quarrels, jealousy, fidelity, and shared philosophy","summary":"The precepts continue with the wife's reserve in speech, the husband's rule over her as the soul governs the body, and marriage as an intimate union of bodies, property and friends. Plutarch advises tact toward the mother-in-law, silence before a husband's anger, reconciliation in the bedchamber rather than sleeping apart, and deafness to slanderers who feed jealousy. Both partners are bound to fidelity; the husband must not demand restraint he does not practise himself. He closes by urging Pollianus to study philosophy and teach it to his wife, and Eurydice to wear the sayings of the wise as ornaments that cost nothing.","ideas":["Marriage of lovers is a fused whole, not a mere joining","Quarrels in the bedchamber are the hardest to heal","The husband must model the restraint he expects","Learning protects women from superstition and folly"],"terms":[{"t":"Aglaonice","d":"Thessalian woman who, knowing eclipse times, made women believe she could draw down the moon."}]},{"i":1445,"j":1478,"loc":"Dinner of the Seven Wise Men 1–4 (146–150)","title":"Diocles sets the scene at Periander's banquet","summary":"Diocles, a seer, tells Nicarchus the true story of Periander's dinner near Lechaeum. On the walk there Neiloxenus of Naucratis brings a sealed problem from Amasis for Bias, and Thales, praised for measuring the pyramid by its shadow, speaks on despots and on how a guest should prepare his character for dinner. They meet Cleobulina (Eumetis) and Anacharsis, the angry Alexidemus who leaves over his place at table, and a portentous horse-child that Thales explains away with a joke to Periander. The guests take their places, Aesop tells a fable of the proud mule, and Diocles notes the plain fare.","ideas":["A guest's fitting adornment is a prepared character","Rank at table should not matter","Thales treats a portent with practical common sense","Wise company makes lavish expense unnecessary"],"terms":[{"t":"Eumetis / Cleobulina","d":"Cleobulus's daughter, famed for riddles and praised for statesmanlike sense."}]},{"i":1479,"j":1514,"loc":"Dinner of the Seven Wise Men 5–7 (150–152)","title":"The ocean riddle and the wise ruler","summary":"After the libations Anacharsis mocks Greek worship with flutes, and Aesop and Neiloxenus discuss flutes made of asses' bones. Neiloxenus reads Amasis's letter: the Ethiopian king has challenged him to drink up the ocean. Bias answers that the Ethiopian must first stop the rivers flowing into it, and Chilon advises Amasis to make his rule sweet to his subjects. Each sage then states what makes a ruler most honoured; Periander complains that their answers keep sensible men from ruling, and Solon and Aesop spar over one ruler against the rule of law.","ideas":["Bias's solution: stop the rivers before drinking the sea","A good ruler is loved, not feared","Solon: in a democracy the one ruler is the law"],"terms":[]},{"i":1515,"j":1561,"loc":"Dinner of the Seven Wise Men 8–11 (152–154)","title":"Questions of Amasis and the best democracy","summary":"Neiloxenus reads the Ethiopian's answers to Amasis's questions on the oldest, greatest, wisest and other superlatives. Thales finds fault with them and gives his own: God is oldest, space greatest, the universe most beautiful, time wisest, hope most common, necessity strongest. Periander recalls the contest of Homer and Hesiod at Chalcis, and Aesop defends Cleobulina's riddles against Cleodorus. At Mnesiphilus's request each sage then names the best popular government, and Periander concludes that they all favour a democracy most like an aristocracy.","ideas":["Thales corrects the Ethiopian's superlatives","Riddling contests have an ancient and serious pedigree","Each sage defines the best democracy"],"terms":[{"t":"anankē (necessity)","d":"Named by Thales as strongest because it alone cannot be overcome."}]},{"i":1562,"j":1583,"loc":"Dinner of the Seven Wise Men 12–13 (154–157)","title":"The best household and Solon's divine tasks","summary":"Diocles asks how a house should be managed. Anacharsis answers Aesop's jibe about his wagon by arguing that a home is its people, not its walls, and the others give brief definitions of the best household. As the cup passes, the company teases Solon for not drinking, and Pittacus and Anacharsis spar over drunkenness. Mnesiphilus explains that for Solon the work of Aphrodite, Dionysus and the Muses is the friendship they create, not intercourse or wine, so among wise men conversation replaces drinking.","ideas":["A home is its family, friends and servants, not its building","The task of an art is its end, not its means","Among the wise, conversation is the mixing-bowl"],"terms":[{"t":"erga theia (divine tasks)","d":"Solon's phrase for the concord and friendship the gods produce through pleasure and wine."}]},{"i":1584,"j":1610,"loc":"Dinner of the Seven Wise Men 14–16 (157–160)","title":"Sufficient wealth and the debate on food","summary":"Chersias raises the measure of sufficient property; Cleobulus says no measure fits a foolish man, whose wants change like the moon. Talk of Epimenides' no-hunger drug leads to Hesiod's mallow and asphodel and the praise of simple food. Solon suggests that needing no food would be the greatest good; Cleodorus and Diocles defend food as the basis of the table, hospitality, agriculture, sacrifice and the body itself. Solon answers that the body's need for food makes us wrong other living things and enslaves the soul, which would, if freed, live for itself and the truth.","ideas":["Desire, not law, sets no limit to a fool's wants","Food grounds hospitality, farming and worship","Solon: freed from nourishment the soul turns to truth"],"terms":[{"t":"autarkeia (self-sufficiency)","d":"For Solon, needing nothing from outside is the only way to avoid wrongdoing in eating."}]},{"i":1611,"j":1640,"loc":"Dinner of the Seven Wise Men 17–21 (160–164)","title":"Arion, the dolphins, and divine providence","summary":"Periander's brother Gorgus arrives from Taenarum and tells how dolphins brought Arion the harper ashore, Arion having leapt from a ship whose sailors meant to murder him; the culprits are arrested. Solon tells of dolphins carrying Hesiod's corpse to Rhium, which exposed his killers, and Pittacus of Enalus and the maiden saved at Lesbos. Anacharsis argues that the soul is God's instrument and living creatures serve his purposes, and Chersias recalls the infant Cypselus's escape. After talk of the Delphic precepts in Homer, Solon proposes a libation, and the party ends.","ideas":["Arion's rescue seen as guided by God","Dolphins' kindness to the living and the dead","The soul is God's most adaptable instrument","Avoid both credulity and haphazard disbelief"],"terms":[{"t":"\"Avoid extremes\" (mēden agan)","d":"Delphic precept, applied by Pittacus to judging marvellous stories."}]},{"i":1641,"j":1677,"loc":"Superstition 1–5 (164–167)","title":"Superstition as fear that never rests","summary":"Plutarch divides ignorance about the gods into atheism in hard characters and superstition in tender ones, and holds that false judgement joined with emotion does the most harm. Atheism is false reasoning that leads to indifference; superstition is an emotion born of false reasoning, a fear that the gods cause harm. This fear follows a man everywhere, poisons even sleep with terrifying dreams, drives him to impostors and degrading rites, turns the gods' shrines into places of dread, and extends its terrors past death. Atheism is a blindness, but superstition is like madness, which sees loved ones as enemies.","ideas":["Atheism is false reason; superstition is false reason plus emotion","The superstitious man fears all things, even sleep","Superstition carries its terrors beyond death"],"terms":[{"t":"deisidaimonia (superstition)","d":"Literally dread of deities: belief that the gods exist but cause pain and injury."},{"t":"atheotēs (atheism)","d":"The judgement that nothing blessed or incorruptible exists, ending in indifference."}]},{"i":1678,"j":1705,"loc":"Superstition 6–8 (167–169)","title":"Atheist and superstitious man facing misfortune","summary":"Plutarch compares the two in adversity. The atheist blames Fortune or looks for causes in his own conduct and seeks remedies; the superstitious man takes every illness or loss as a punishment from God, turns away physician and philosopher, and wallows in mud and penances. Examples follow of Midas and Aristodemus, who killed themselves over omens, Nicias paralysed by an eclipse, and the Jews who would not fight on the Sabbath. By contrast the pilot, Hesiod's farmer, Ajax and Agamemnon pray while acting, for God is brave hope, not cowardly excuse.","ideas":["The superstitious man refuses help, fearing to resist God","Superstition turns small ills into disasters","Prayer should accompany action, not replace it"],"terms":[]},{"i":1706,"j":1735,"loc":"Superstition 9–14 (169–171)","title":"Which is the greater impiety","summary":"Even at festivals the superstitious man cannot rejoice and approaches temples as if they were dens of beasts. Plutarch asks why atheism is called impious but superstition is not, since it credits the gods with cruelty and spite, saying he would rather be thought never born than be thought such a person. The superstitious man hates and fears the gods while worshipping them, would gladly be an atheist, and by his absurd rites gives atheism its excuse; human sacrifices among Gauls, Scythians and Carthaginians are worse than having no gods. The range ends as he turns to escaping superstition without falling into atheism, overleaping true religion between them.","ideas":["Thinking meanly of the gods is worse than denying them","Superstition breeds and excuses atheism","Human sacrifice shows superstition at its worst","True religion lies between the two errors"],"terms":[{"t":"eusebeia (true religion)","d":"The mean between atheism and superstition that some overleap in fleeing one extreme."}]}]});